    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, I'd be happy to help with that! Mitosis is the process by which a eukaryotic cell splits into two identical daughter cells. It consists of distinct phases: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase. In prophase, the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, and the nuclear envelope breaks down. Metaphase follows, where chromosomes line up along the equatorial plate of the cell. During anaphase, the sister chromatids are pulled apart by spindle fibers to opposite poles of the cell. Finally, in telophase, the chromosomes de-condense, the nuclear envelope reforms, and the cell begins to divide through cytokinesis, leading to two genetically identical daughter cells. Mitosis ensures that each new cell has the same number of chromosomes and is an essential process for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction in many organisms. I hope this helps you with your study guide! Let me know if you have any more questions.",
      "source": "target",
