    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, happy to refine it for a better score! Mitosis is crucial in biology because it's the process by which a single cell divides to form two identical daughter cells, ensuring that every new cell contains the same genetic information. Learning about mitosis helps students understand how organisms grow, develop, and repair tissues. So, let's dive into the phases. In prophase, when the cell begins the mitotic process, the chromosomes condense and become visible, and the nuclear envelope starts to disintegrate, which prepares the cell for the division. Once we move to metaphase, the chromosomes align in the middle of the cell at what’s known as the equatorial plate, ensuring that each daughter cell will have an equal share of genetic material.\n\nIn the next phase, anaphase, the sister chromatids are pulled apart toward opposite poles thanks to the spindle fibers. This happens so that each pole of the cell ends up with the same number of chromosomes, and when the cell enters telophase, the chromosomes finally begin to de-condense, the nuclear envelope reforms, and the cell starts to split in two during cytokinesis. Remembering these steps—a helpful way to understand the flow of mitosis—isn't just critical for exams; it",
